1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,074
Two in the morning, the thirty-first of December, nineteen seventy-eight.

2
00:00:05,074 --> 00:00:10,401
A cargo plane is crossing the sea off New Zealand with a television crew on board.

3
00:00:10,401 --> 00:00:14,233
Outside the window, bright lights are keeping pace with it.

4
00:00:14,699 --> 00:00:16,193
The camera is rolling.

5
00:00:16,193 --> 00:00:18,230
The pilots can see the lights.

6
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:22,169
Wellington air traffic control can see something on radar.

7
00:00:22,169 --> 00:00:25,699
And the whole thing is about to go around the world.

8
00:00:26,166 --> 00:00:29,640
Was this an alien escort at thirty thousand feet?

9
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:35,100
Or the most famous case of ordinary things looking extraordinary in the dark?

10
00:00:35,566 --> 00:00:37,841
Tonight we run it as a flight log.

11
00:00:37,841 --> 00:00:40,719
Minute by minute, two flights, one verdict.

12
00:00:40,719 --> 00:00:44,533
Every claim gets checked, and every theory gets labelled.

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:45,776
Facts first.

14
00:00:45,776 --> 00:00:46,941
Then the theories.

15
00:00:46,941 --> 00:00:48,299
Cleared for take-off.

16
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:53,554
Chapter one.

17
00:00:53,554 --> 00:00:54,669
The first flight.

18
00:00:54,669 --> 00:01:05,366
Late on the twentieth of December, nineteen seventy-eight, a Safe Air freighter, an Argosy, is flying freight over Cook Strait between Wellington and Christchurch.

19
00:01:05,833 --> 00:01:12,392
Captain Vern Powell and First Officer Ian Pirie see strange lights off the coast near Kaikoura.

20
00:01:12,392 --> 00:01:14,671
They watch them for a long while.

21
00:01:14,671 --> 00:01:19,366
The lights seem to follow the aircraft, twenty or thirty miles away.

22
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:23,404
Wellington radar shows returns in the same area.

23
00:01:23,404 --> 00:01:33,299
At an air base at Woodbourne, a Royal New Zealand Air Force officer, Warrant Officer Ian Uffindell, also sees lights from the ground.

24
00:01:33,766 --> 00:01:34,818
Word leaks out.

25
00:01:34,818 --> 00:01:41,407
By the next day, newspapers are asking if something strange is flying over the Kaikoura coast.

26
00:01:41,407 --> 00:01:46,033
One reporter, an Australian, decides to go and see it for himself.

27
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,487
His name is Quentin Fogarty.

28
00:01:48,487 --> 00:01:51,256
He works for Channel Zero in Melbourne.

29
00:01:51,256 --> 00:01:58,000
With a cameraman, David Crockett, and Crockett's wife Ngaire on sound, he flies to New Zealand.

30
00:01:58,466 --> 00:01:59,866
The plan is simple.

31
00:01:59,866 --> 00:02:02,591
Fly the same route, at the same hour.

32
00:02:02,591 --> 00:02:04,285
Film whatever turns up.

33
00:02:04,285 --> 00:02:06,199
Nobody expects it to work.

34
00:02:06,666 --> 00:02:07,457
Chapter two.

35
00:02:07,457 --> 00:02:08,644
The second flight.

36
00:02:08,644 --> 00:02:18,599
Just before midnight on the thirtieth of December, the Argosy leaves Wellington, with Captain Bill Startup and First Officer Bob Guard at the controls.

37
00:02:19,066 --> 00:02:20,938
For a while there is nothing.

38
00:02:20,938 --> 00:02:22,746
Then the crew report lights.

39
00:02:22,746 --> 00:02:28,363
Later, near twenty past two in the morning, the lights return, brighter, off the coast.

40
00:02:28,363 --> 00:02:31,333
The crew watch them for more than ten minutes.

41
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:34,000
Crockett films through the window.

42
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,070
Fogarty talks to the microphone.

43
00:02:36,070 --> 00:02:42,800
In the recording he says the line that will become the title of his book: let us hope they are friendly.

44
00:02:43,266 --> 00:02:47,211
Captain Startup turns the plane toward one of the lights.

45
00:02:47,211 --> 00:02:49,702
As he does, it appears to move away.

46
00:02:49,702 --> 00:02:55,099
Later he will say it behaved like nothing he had seen in a long flying career.

47
00:02:55,566 --> 00:02:58,004
They land at Christchurch at dawn.

48
00:02:58,004 --> 00:03:00,514
The film is rushed to be developed.

49
00:03:00,514 --> 00:03:06,466
Within days it is on television in Australia, then America, then almost everywhere.

50
00:03:06,933 --> 00:03:12,683
The footage is blurry, but vivid: glowing lights, flares, shapes that seem to pulse.

51
00:03:12,683 --> 00:03:16,312
It is exactly what people imagine a UFO to look like.

52
00:03:16,312 --> 00:03:19,666
And that, as we will see, is part of the problem.

53
00:03:20,133 --> 00:03:23,154
Before we judge, let us collect the evidence.

54
00:03:23,154 --> 00:03:24,966
Five claims, one at a time.

55
00:03:25,433 --> 00:03:26,505
Chapter three.

56
00:03:26,505 --> 00:03:27,883
The official file.

57
00:03:27,883 --> 00:03:32,936
The New Zealand Prime Minister, Robert Muldoon, takes an interest.

58
00:03:32,936 --> 00:03:40,133
The Air Force opens an investigation, the only New Zealand UFO case ever investigated in full.

59
00:03:40,599 --> 00:03:51,533
Air Force officers, civil aviation staff and scientists from the government research department, the DSIR, go through the reports and the film, frame by frame.

60
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,577
Their report goes to the United Nations in January nineteen seventy-nine.

61
00:03:57,577 --> 00:03:59,716
The conclusion is not alien.

62
00:03:59,716 --> 00:04:05,599
It is a combination of ordinary causes, and the report names them one by one.

63
00:04:06,066 --> 00:04:16,466
Those causes include the planet Venus, bright car and train lights on the coast, meteors, odd effects of radar, and a fleet of fishing boats far offshore.

64
00:04:16,933 --> 00:04:23,204
The files were made public in December twenty-ten, more than thirty years after the flights.

65
00:04:23,204 --> 00:04:25,522
Reading them is surprisingly fair.

66
00:04:25,522 --> 00:04:29,066
The investigators admit what they could not explain.

67
00:04:29,533 --> 00:04:31,600
So let us test the first suspect.

68
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:33,011
Chapter four.

69
00:04:33,011 --> 00:04:34,392
Suspect one, Venus.

70
00:04:34,392 --> 00:04:40,425
Venus can be extremely bright, and near the horizon it flickers and changes colour.

71
00:04:40,425 --> 00:04:42,533
Pilots have chased it before.

72
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,463
Some of the first-flight sightings fit that very well.

73
00:04:46,463 --> 00:04:51,786
A bright light low in the east, seen from a moving aeroplane, appears to follow it.

74
00:04:51,786 --> 00:04:53,133
That is how it works.

75
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,593
But the film sighting has a timing problem.

76
00:04:56,593 --> 00:05:00,701
The lights were seen around two twenty-five in the morning.

77
00:05:00,701 --> 00:05:03,416
Venus rose at about three twenty-eight.

78
00:05:03,416 --> 00:05:04,600
An hour too late.

79
00:05:05,066 --> 00:05:08,498
So Venus explains some of the lights, but not all.

80
00:05:08,498 --> 00:05:10,283
The investigators knew it.

81
00:05:10,283 --> 00:05:11,724
The sceptics know it.

82
00:05:11,724 --> 00:05:14,333
Which is why the next suspect matters.

83
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:15,655
Suspect two.

84
00:05:15,655 --> 00:05:16,866
Out on the water.

85
00:05:17,333 --> 00:05:18,238
Chapter five.

86
00:05:18,238 --> 00:05:20,258
Suspect two, the squid boats.

87
00:05:20,258 --> 00:05:23,601
Squid are lured at night by huge banks of lamps.

88
00:05:23,601 --> 00:05:31,333
In that season, about fifty Japanese squid boats were fishing roughly a hundred and twenty miles off the coast.

89
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:36,199
From the air, a boat under those lamps is a brilliant white blaze.

90
00:05:36,199 --> 00:05:38,466
It can look like a hovering craft.

91
00:05:38,466 --> 00:05:43,399
Under atmospheric conditions, the light can be bent, lifted and stretched.

92
00:05:43,866 --> 00:05:48,846
A DSIR scientist, William Ireland, later compared the film with the boats.

93
00:05:48,846 --> 00:05:54,566
He matched the pattern of lights to vessels roughly six kilometres from the aircraft.

94
00:05:55,033 --> 00:05:57,610
The DSIR also tested Crockett's window.

95
00:05:57,610 --> 00:06:03,491
Shining a torch at the glass, they reproduced the flaring lights that appear in the film.

96
00:06:03,491 --> 00:06:06,266
A reflection, in other words, not a craft.

97
00:06:06,733 --> 00:06:08,812
Any pilot knows this problem.

98
00:06:08,812 --> 00:06:12,757
A lit cabin and a dark sky turn a window into a mirror.

99
00:06:12,757 --> 00:06:17,633
A film made through glass, at night, is a poor measuring instrument.

100
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:21,048
Captain Startup and Fogarty rejected this.

101
00:06:21,048 --> 00:06:27,295
The boats, they said, were too far away, and what they saw moved in ways boats could not.

102
00:06:27,295 --> 00:06:30,033
Honest witnesses, honestly disagreeing.

103
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:33,626
Still, the crew were not the only ones watching.

104
00:06:33,626 --> 00:06:36,166
The next suspect is not a light at all.

105
00:06:36,633 --> 00:06:37,484
Chapter six.

106
00:06:37,484 --> 00:06:39,258
Suspect three, the radar.

107
00:06:39,258 --> 00:06:42,592
Wellington radar showed returns near the plane.

108
00:06:42,592 --> 00:06:47,133
For many people, that settled it: something solid was out there.

109
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:49,414
But radar is not a camera.

110
00:06:49,414 --> 00:06:58,833
Under the right atmospheric conditions, beams bend and bounce, producing echoes from the sea, from distant ships or from layers of air.

111
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:04,351
The aircraft's own radar was also in a mode designed to map the ground.

112
00:07:04,351 --> 00:07:08,833
That mode can show bright returns that are not aircraft at all.

113
00:07:09,300 --> 00:07:13,191
Air traffic controller Bill Frame reported what he saw.

114
00:07:13,191 --> 00:07:18,357
But the investigators noted the returns did not track the lights exactly.

115
00:07:18,357 --> 00:07:21,966
Venus, they added, does not appear on radar at all.

116
00:07:22,433 --> 00:07:25,457
This is the part that keeps the case alive.

117
00:07:25,457 --> 00:07:28,411
Not one single factor explains everything.

118
00:07:28,411 --> 00:07:35,233
Several mundane things at once could, but they are harder to prove than a single dramatic answer.

119
00:07:35,699 --> 00:07:41,541
The investigators concluded that the radar data supported the natural explanation.

120
00:07:41,541 --> 00:07:42,966
Not everyone agreed.

121
00:07:43,433 --> 00:07:44,462
Chapter seven.

122
00:07:44,462 --> 00:07:45,490
The dissenter.

123
00:07:45,490 --> 00:07:51,368
An American optical physicist, Bruce Maccabee, studied the film and the reports.

124
00:07:51,368 --> 00:07:57,099
He argued that at least one object was real, solid, and about five miles away.

125
00:07:57,566 --> 00:08:01,721
He suggested it may have been as large as a two-storey building.

126
00:08:01,721 --> 00:08:06,266
If he is right, it was far too big and too close to be a fishing boat.

127
00:08:06,733 --> 00:08:14,053
The New Zealand investigators, and later sceptics such as those at the New Zealand Skeptics, disagreed.

128
00:08:14,053 --> 00:08:15,901
Their answer was the same.

129
00:08:15,901 --> 00:08:19,100
Several ordinary causes, misread in the dark.

130
00:08:19,566 --> 00:08:28,975
When the files were released in twenty-ten, a New Zealand paper summed up the official finding with a headline saying the UFOs were all natural.

131
00:08:28,975 --> 00:08:32,700
But as we have seen, the story is more careful than that.

132
00:08:33,166 --> 00:08:35,402
Fogarty never fully accepted it.

133
00:08:35,402 --> 00:08:36,799
Neither did Startup.

134
00:08:36,799 --> 00:08:42,108
For them, what they felt in that cockpit was real, whatever the explanation.

135
00:08:42,108 --> 00:08:44,833
And that, too, is part of the evidence.

136
00:08:45,299 --> 00:08:46,466
Now the debrief.

137
00:08:46,933 --> 00:08:47,509
Debrief.

138
00:08:47,509 --> 00:08:49,166
Time to score Kaikoura.

139
00:08:49,633 --> 00:08:53,081
Honest witnesses seeing real lights: evidence.

140
00:08:53,081 --> 00:08:59,004
Radar returns on ground and aircraft radar: partial, with known natural causes.

141
00:08:59,004 --> 00:09:04,176
The film: weak, since a window reflection reproduces its main flares.

142
00:09:04,176 --> 00:09:08,000
The Venus explanation: partial, it fails on timing.

143
00:09:08,466 --> 00:09:12,831
The squid boat match: evidence, but contested by the crew.

144
00:09:12,831 --> 00:09:17,723
An object five miles away and as large as a building: unverified.

145
00:09:17,723 --> 00:09:20,433
Extraterrestrial craft: no evidence.

146
00:09:20,899 --> 00:09:21,759
The verdict.

147
00:09:21,759 --> 00:09:24,196
Not alien, but not trivial either.

148
00:09:24,196 --> 00:09:32,151
Venus, boats, a window and radar quirks together explain most of what was seen, though not every single detail.

149
00:09:32,151 --> 00:09:36,666
A fair verdict is natural causes, with a few honest loose ends.

150
00:09:37,133 --> 00:09:39,572
What stays with you is the lesson.

151
00:09:39,572 --> 00:09:45,956
At night, from a moving plane, even trained pilots can misjudge distance, speed and size.

152
00:09:45,956 --> 00:09:47,534
That is not a failure.

153
00:09:47,534 --> 00:09:49,399
It is how human eyes work.

154
00:09:49,866 --> 00:09:54,500
Next case: the Taos Hum, a low sound that only some people hear.

155
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:58,700
Is it a secret machine, or a mystery inside the human ear?

156
00:09:59,166 --> 00:10:01,296
What do you think those lights were?

157
00:10:01,296 --> 00:10:06,266
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
